More details found:
It's  special only for timestamp column type. if your 

```
set @@explicit_defaults_for_timestamp=0;
create table t1 (a timestamp NOT NULL);
insert into t1 values (NULL);   // converted to the current timestamp

set @@explicit_defaults_for_timestamp=1;
insert into t1 values (NULL);   // convertion is controlled by explicit_defaults_for_timestamp
```
while no matter whether explicit_defaults_for_timestamp is on or not, column type change from NULL-> timestamp NOT NULL can always succeed.
```
create table t(a int);
insert into t values(null);
set @@explicit_defaults_for_timestamp=1;
alter table t modify a timestamp  not NULL;   // succeed

create table t(a int);
insert into t values(null);
set @@explicit_defaults_for_timestamp=0;
alter table t modify a timestamp  not NULL;   // succeed
```
